WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.884
This is a technical deep dive on
Infoblox DDI for federal agencies: DNS,

2
00:00:05.884 --> 00:00:10.511
DHCP and IP address management,
delivered on Infoblox's

3
00:00:10.511 --> 00:00:15.238
NIOS Grid and on its cloud-managed
service, Universal DDI.

4
00:00:15.238 --> 00:00:19.372
It's for the architects and engineers
who will decide whether to build an

5
00:00:19.372 --> 00:00:23.438
agency's name and address layer on
Infoblox, and how to run it once they do.

6
00:00:23.438 --> 00:00:27.063
After a five-minute orientation,
it comes in two parts of about

7
00:00:27.063 --> 00:00:30.901
twenty-two minutes each, in four
modules you can watch on their own.

8
00:00:30.901 --> 00:00:33.690
Part 1 covers the platform,
then the case for it:

9
00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:38.683
which federal requirements it answers,
what its FedRAMP listing actually covers,

10
00:00:38.683 --> 00:00:43.749
how it compares with BlueCat, EfficientIP,
Microsoft and the native cloud services,

11
00:00:43.749 --> 00:00:46.613
and the risks you have to
manage if you choose it.

12
00:00:46.613 --> 00:00:50.561
Part 2 covers divisions: how
a central team hands DNS,

13
00:00:50.561 --> 00:00:56.622
DHCP and address management to bureaus
and offices, on NIOS and on Universal DDI,

14
00:00:56.622 --> 00:01:00.939
without losing control, and it
ends with a worked example,

15
00:01:00.939 --> 00:01:04.612
a responsibility matrix
and a rollout sequence.

16
00:01:04.612 --> 00:01:05.924
Two ground rules.

17
00:01:05.924 --> 00:01:11.962
Every fact here comes from a primary source
opened on October twenty-second, 2026.

18
00:01:11.962 --> 00:01:14.773
And anything that is
only Infoblox marketing

19
00:01:14.773 --> 00:01:17.213
is called a vendor claim when it appears.

20
00:01:17.213 --> 00:01:21.855
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x
and Universal DDI docs;

21
00:01:21.855 --> 00:01:28.082
FedRAMP Marketplace products.json;
NIST CMVP; DISA STIG file store;

22
00:01:28.082 --> 00:01:35.340
OMB and CISA publications; the Federal
Modernization Workbook's Book 08.

23
00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:37.278
Why this matters.

24
00:01:37.278 --> 00:01:41.702
Almost every connection an agency
makes starts with a DNS lookup,

25
00:01:41.702 --> 00:01:47.028
and in a hybrid, multi-cloud estate that
answer decides far more than an address.

26
00:01:47.028 --> 00:01:51.065
DHCP and IP address management
sit right beside it.

27
00:01:51.065 --> 00:01:57.378
NIST's Secure DNS Deployment Guide,
SP 800-81 Revision 3, tells agencies

28
00:01:57.378 --> 00:02:03.365
to keep DHCP lease history so an IP
address can be mapped to an asset.

29
00:02:03.365 --> 00:02:06.848
And the address plan is the
record behind everything the

30
00:02:06.848 --> 00:02:10.628
inventory directives ask for,
which we'll see on the next slide.

31
00:02:10.628 --> 00:02:12.971
That's why this deck treats DNS,

32
00:02:12.971 --> 00:02:17.939
DHCP and IPAM as one control
plane rather than three services.

33
00:02:17.939 --> 00:02:21.878
The Federal Modernization
Workbook's Book 08 of the workbook

34
00:02:21.878 --> 00:02:25.352
describes what that plane is
made of, and it isn't one box.

35
00:02:25.352 --> 00:02:30.645
There's an authoritative and recursive
layer on premises that hosts internal zones,

36
00:02:30.645 --> 00:02:34.577
answers clients, and applies
response policy zones and DNSSEC.

37
00:02:34.577 --> 00:02:39.150
There's a management plane, more and
more often delivered as a cloud service,

38
00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:41.657
that holds the system of record for zones,

39
00:02:41.657 --> 00:02:44.828
forwarding rules, address
data and security policy.

40
00:02:44.828 --> 00:02:48.164
And there are local resolvers
and agents that keep answering

41
00:02:48.164 --> 00:02:51.239
from local state if the management
plane can't be reached.

42
00:02:51.239 --> 00:02:55.665
Cloud DNS isn't replaced;
it's managed as an overlay.

43
00:02:55.665 --> 00:02:58.040
So here is the argument
of the whole session.

44
00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:01.453
Federal rules require
capabilities, not products.

45
00:03:01.453 --> 00:03:06.965
Those capabilities, taken together, need
one authoritative and governed DDI plane.

46
00:03:06.965 --> 00:03:10.707
Infoblox is one of the strongest
ways to deliver that plane,

47
00:03:10.707 --> 00:03:14.678
with evidence, and it also carries
real risks you have to manage.

48
00:03:14.678 --> 00:03:15.828
We'll cover both.

49
00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:24.630
Sources: NIST SP 800-81r3;
Book 08, Chapters 01 and 08.

50
00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:29.280
Here is what the rules actually
require, stated as capabilities.

51
00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:37.156
DNSSEC: SC-20 in the low and moderate
baselines means signing agency zones,

52
00:03:37.156 --> 00:03:41.416
and OMB M-08-23, from
2008, is still in force;

53
00:03:41.416 --> 00:03:47.355
we checked every 2025 and 2026
memo, and none rescinds it.

54
00:03:47.355 --> 00:03:52.030
SC-21 means validating answers
on recursive resolvers.

55
00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:58.405
SC-22 asks for fault-tolerant DNS with
internal and external roles separated.

56
00:03:58.405 --> 00:04:02.247
TIC 3.0, version 3.3, carries signing and

57
00:04:02.247 --> 00:04:06.455
validation as policy
enforcement point capabilities.

58
00:04:06.455 --> 00:04:13.917
Encrypted DNS: M-22-09 says agencies must
resolve DNS using encrypted DNS wherever

59
00:04:13.917 --> 00:04:19.018
it's technically supported, and
Executive Order 14144, section 4(c),

60
00:04:19.018 --> 00:04:22.513
which the June 2025
amendment left standing,

61
00:04:22.513 --> 00:04:29.030
repeats the push and adds contract language
for any product that acts as a resolver.

62
00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:31.972
CISA's Protective DNS sits upstream:

63
00:04:31.972 --> 00:04:37.192
internal resolvers forward to it,
and the agency keeps its own logs.

64
00:04:37.192 --> 00:04:38.454
Logging is subtle.

65
00:04:38.454 --> 00:04:48.355
M-26-14, from May 2026, rescinded
M-21-31 and doesn't mention DNS anywhere.

66
00:04:48.355 --> 00:04:54.440
What carries DNS and DHCP logging forward
is CISA's Logging Reference Architecture

67
00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:57.439
from August, which maps
the old DNS and DHCP

68
00:04:57.439 --> 00:05:00.867
categories into its
network-activity baseline.

69
00:05:00.867 --> 00:05:04.530
Retention is six months searchable
and twelve retrievable.

70
00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:12.807
Inventory: BOD 23-01 wants discovery of
all agency IPv4 space every seven days,

71
00:05:12.807 --> 00:05:17.205
and BOD 26-04, which
replaced 19-02 and 22-01,

72
00:05:17.205 --> 00:05:23.930
wants public IP addresses and
domains attested every quarter.

73
00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:30.030
M-21-07 set IPv6-only
targets that have all passed.

74
00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:33.855
And SC-13 wants validated cryptography.

75
00:05:33.855 --> 00:05:35.543
Now the honest starting point.

76
00:05:35.543 --> 00:05:41.818
No rule names Infoblox or any DDI vendor,
and nothing requires a DDI platform.

77
00:05:41.818 --> 00:05:48.105
Windows DNS, BIND, or cloud DNS with a
separate IPAM can meet these rules too.

78
00:05:48.105 --> 00:05:50.855
Choosing a platform is an
architecture decision.

79
00:05:50.855 --> 00:06:04.520
Sources: NIST SP 800-53r5 OSCAL 5.2.0;
OMB M-08-23, M-22-09, M-26-14, M-21-07;

80
00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:17.744
EO 14144, EO 14306; CISA Protective
DNS, Logging Reference Architecture,

81
00:06:17.744 --> 00:06:27.140
TIC 3.0 v3.3, BOD 23-01,
BOD 26-04; NIST CMVP.

82
00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:29.828
Module 1: the platform.

83
00:06:29.828 --> 00:06:32.384
Before arguing for or against Infoblox,

84
00:06:32.384 --> 00:06:35.948
we need an accurate picture of
what it is in October 2026,

85
00:06:35.948 --> 00:06:40.441
because the names have changed and a
lot of older material is now wrong.

86
00:06:40.441 --> 00:06:45.684
We'll start with the NIOS Grid, the
on-premises architecture most agencies know,

87
00:06:45.684 --> 00:06:50.120
then the cloud-managed side,
Universal DDI and the Infoblox Portal,

88
00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:53.428
including the limits
Infoblox itself documents.

89
00:06:53.428 --> 00:06:58.316
Then DNS, DHCP and IPAM in turn,
the Threat Defense security layer,

90
00:06:58.316 --> 00:07:03.827
and finally the automation interfaces
and the logging and reporting path.

91
00:07:03.827 --> 00:07:07.679
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x admin guide;

92
00:07:07.679 --> 00:07:12.970
Universal DDI and Threat
Defense documentation.

93
00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:18.477
The NIOS Grid is a group of appliances
that share sections of one distributed

94
00:07:18.477 --> 00:07:23.532
database, configured and monitored through
a single access point, the Grid Master.

95
00:07:23.532 --> 00:07:27.967
The Grid Master can be one appliance
or a high-availability pair, and every

96
00:07:27.967 --> 00:07:30.510
administrator reaches the GUI through it,

97
00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:33.345
even administrators scoped
to a single member.

98
00:07:33.345 --> 00:07:39.820
Members can be physical Infoblox hardware
or virtual vNIOS, singly or in HA pairs.

99
00:07:39.820 --> 00:07:45.839
The Grid Master talks to every member
hub-and-spoke, over encrypted VPN tunnels

100
00:07:45.839 --> 00:07:51.857
on UDP 1194 by default, after a
challenge-response authentication on port 2114.

101
00:07:51.857 --> 00:07:55.858
A Grid Master Candidate holds a
complete copy of the database.

102
00:07:55.858 --> 00:08:00.196
Promoting it is a manual operation, and
you can't do it from the cloud portal.

103
00:08:00.196 --> 00:08:03.698
HA pairs share one virtual
IP and virtual MAC,

104
00:08:03.698 --> 00:08:07.107
with VRRP advertisements as the heartbeat.

105
00:08:07.107 --> 00:08:12.908
A Grid can run IPv4-only,
IPv6-only or dual mode.

106
00:08:12.908 --> 00:08:14.095
Form factors.

107
00:08:14.095 --> 00:08:20.320
The current hardware line is Trinzic
X6, supported from NIOS 9.0.1.

108
00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:25.451
vNIOS for VMware runs from four virtual
CPUs and thirty-two gigabytes up to

109
00:08:25.451 --> 00:08:30.995
thirty-two CPUs and three hundred eighty-four,
and every model can be a Grid Master.

110
00:08:30.995 --> 00:08:37.048
vNIOS for Azure supports HA from 9.0.4
and explicitly covers Azure Government;

111
00:08:37.048 --> 00:08:43.195
note that Azure doesn't support running
DHCP in Azure for on-premises networks.

112
00:08:43.195 --> 00:08:49.695
vNIOS for AWS can be a Grid Master in HA,
and its vDiscovery has a GovCloud option.

113
00:08:49.695 --> 00:08:55.533
Google Cloud and Oracle Cloud are
covered too, with OCI HA new in 9.1.

114
00:08:55.533 --> 00:09:00.532
NIOS-X servers are the data plane
for Universal DDI, which is next.

115
00:09:00.532 --> 00:09:08.182
Versions: NIOS 9.1.0 went GA in
March 2026 and ships BIND 9.20.

116
00:09:08.182 --> 00:09:11.274
NIOS 9.0.6 introduced a Long-Term Support

117
00:09:11.274 --> 00:09:14.833
train with up to twenty-four
months of support.

118
00:09:14.833 --> 00:09:22.522
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x About Grids,
Promoting a Grid Master Candidate,

119
00:09:22.522 --> 00:09:28.069
About HA Failover, 9.1.0 What's
New; vNIOS install guides;

120
00:09:28.069 --> 00:09:35.860
Supported Platforms for NIOS-X Servers;
Infoblox NIOS 9.0.6 and 9.1.0 blogs.

121
00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:39.748
Universal DDI is the cloud-managed side.

122
00:09:39.748 --> 00:09:45.421
It used to be called BloxOne DDI;
Infoblox renamed it in September 2024,

123
00:09:45.421 --> 00:09:49.236
and the on-premises hosts
became NIOS-X servers.

124
00:09:49.236 --> 00:09:55.289
The old Cloud Services Portal is now the
Infoblox Portal, although the documentation

125
00:09:55.289 --> 00:10:00.235
still uses both names and the API host
is still csp dot infoblox dot com.

126
00:10:00.235 --> 00:10:04.884
The data plane is NIOS-X servers that
you run, or NIOS-X as a Service,

127
00:10:04.884 --> 00:10:10.323
which Infoblox runs from its own points
of presence in AWS and Google Cloud.

128
00:10:10.323 --> 00:10:15.570
The management plane reaches
outward to Route 53, Azure DNS,

129
00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:21.673
Google Cloud DNS, Cloudflare and
Akamai, and to Microsoft DNS and DHCP.

130
00:10:21.673 --> 00:10:25.454
It can also manage NIOS
Grids from inside the Portal,

131
00:10:25.454 --> 00:10:31.172
and the documented hybrid DNS pattern
makes NIOS primary and NIOS-X secondary.

132
00:10:31.172 --> 00:10:35.565
Infoblox markets this as a single
cloud-based control plane and API;

133
00:10:35.565 --> 00:10:40.272
that's a vendor claim, and the limits on
the right are why you should test it.

134
00:10:40.272 --> 00:10:43.272
These limits come from
Infoblox's own pages.

135
00:10:43.272 --> 00:10:48.233
Inside the Portal, the embedded NIOS view
can't promote a Grid Master Candidate,

136
00:10:48.233 --> 00:10:53.498
show Splunk reporting or download files,
and Multi-Grid Management isn't supported.

137
00:10:53.498 --> 00:10:58.261
NIOS DHCP failover associations
can't be managed from the Portal.

138
00:10:58.261 --> 00:11:01.335
WAPI Passthrough reaches
only the Grid Master.

139
00:11:01.335 --> 00:11:06.298
NIOS-X as a Service has no IPv6,
no overlapping IP spaces and no

140
00:11:06.298 --> 00:11:11.460
Azure points of presence, which
matters under the IPv6 mandate.

141
00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:16.899
The cloud DNS integrations sync simple
routing records only, without tags,

142
00:11:16.899 --> 00:11:21.460
with NS records read-only and
alias records turned into CNAMEs.

143
00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:24.172
And Google's DNSSEC records don't sync.

144
00:11:24.172 --> 00:11:26.224
So "one console" is overstated:

145
00:11:26.224 --> 00:11:30.584
in practice you'll run Grid Manager
and the Portal side by side.

146
00:11:30.584 --> 00:11:37.482
Sources: Infoblox What's New in Universal
DDI; Limitations of Universal DDI;

147
00:11:37.482 --> 00:11:44.935
Limitations of NIOS-X as a Service; Universal
DDI and NIOS Grid Hybrid Deployment;

148
00:11:44.935 --> 00:11:49.051
Route 53, Azure DNS and
GCP integration pages;

149
00:11:49.051 --> 00:11:53.080
infoblox.com Universal DDI page (vendor).

150
00:11:53.830 --> 00:11:58.755
DNS on NIOS 9.1, with the
catch for each feature.

151
00:11:58.755 --> 00:12:02.273
DNS views serve different
answers to different clients

152
00:12:02.273 --> 00:12:05.567
from match lists of client
or destination addresses.

153
00:12:05.567 --> 00:12:09.097
The first match wins, and you
can set forwarders per view,

154
00:12:09.097 --> 00:12:13.242
which is how you'd point an internal
view at CISA's Protective DNS.

155
00:12:13.242 --> 00:12:16.729
DNSSEC is strong, with
structure you must plan around.

156
00:12:16.729 --> 00:12:19.918
Only the Grid Master can be
primary for a signed zone.

157
00:12:19.918 --> 00:12:24.404
It acts as a hidden primary and
members serve the zone as secondaries.

158
00:12:24.404 --> 00:12:29.420
Keys live either on the Grid Master
or on an external network HSM,

159
00:12:29.420 --> 00:12:32.542
Thales Luna or Entrust nShield, not both.

160
00:12:32.542 --> 00:12:36.953
Zone-signing keys roll automatically
every thirty days by default,

161
00:12:36.953 --> 00:12:40.418
and key-signing keys yearly,
automatically or by hand.

162
00:12:40.418 --> 00:12:45.623
If the parent zone is in the Grid, the new
DS record is inserted for you; otherwise

163
00:12:45.623 --> 00:12:50.668
someone exports it to the parent, which
for a dot-gov zone means the registrar.

164
00:12:50.668 --> 00:12:55.768
DNS Firewall uses response policy
zones: local, feed or FireEye,

165
00:12:55.768 --> 00:13:00.868
up to thirty-two of them, with hits
logged to syslog in CEF format.

166
00:13:00.868 --> 00:13:05.818
GSS-TSIG lets Windows domain controllers
make Kerberos-signed updates,

167
00:13:05.818 --> 00:13:08.706
up to two hundred
fifty-six keys per member.

168
00:13:08.706 --> 00:13:14.705
Grid members can manage Windows DNS
and DHCP servers without an agent,

169
00:13:14.705 --> 00:13:19.980
over MS-RPC, read-only or read-write,
and 9.1 adds IPv6 management.

170
00:13:19.980 --> 00:13:25.592
Encrypted DNS: from 9.1.0,
DNS over TLS and DNS over

171
00:13:25.592 --> 00:13:31.492
HTTPS are served by BIND,
but only on X6 appliances.

172
00:13:31.492 --> 00:13:36.105
Infoblox says they're free on X6
and need an extra license on X5.

173
00:13:36.105 --> 00:13:39.184
One configuration item you must change:

174
00:13:39.184 --> 00:13:42.730
the default DNSSEC
algorithm is RSA with SHA-1.

175
00:13:42.730 --> 00:13:47.955
Pick SHA-256 or an ECDSA curve
before you sign anything.

176
00:13:47.955 --> 00:13:55.892
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x: Configuring
DNS Views, Anycast, DNSSEC on a Grid,

177
00:13:55.892 --> 00:14:03.959
Setting DNSSEC Parameters, Thales Luna
HSM, DNS Firewall, GSS-TSIG, Managing

178
00:14:03.959 --> 00:14:08.774
Microsoft Servers, DoT
and DoH Architecture,

179
00:14:08.774 --> 00:14:12.780
9.1.0 What's New; NIOS
9.1.0 blog (vendor).

180
00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:15.055
DHCP first.

181
00:14:15.055 --> 00:14:20.035
Failover peers form an association over
TCP, split clients by a hash of the

182
00:14:20.035 --> 00:14:25.342
MAC address, fifty-fifty by default, and
send each other lease updates directly.

183
00:14:25.342 --> 00:14:30.068
The Grid Master isn't involved in
failover, whatever older material says.

184
00:14:30.068 --> 00:14:36.467
An association serves ranges in one network
view only, and failover is IPv4 only.

185
00:14:36.467 --> 00:14:39.851
That last point matters
under the IPv6 mandate,

186
00:14:39.851 --> 00:14:43.668
though as we'll see in Module
2, Windows has the same gap.

187
00:14:43.668 --> 00:14:48.647
DHCPv6 itself is supported,
including prefix delegation,

188
00:14:48.647 --> 00:14:51.655
and fixed addresses arrived in 9.0.6.

189
00:14:51.655 --> 00:14:55.990
Fingerprinting matches options
55 and 60 and is on by default,

190
00:14:55.990 --> 00:14:59.730
and filters can deny leases
to device types per range.

191
00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:02.419
Lease history is archived and exportable,

192
00:15:02.419 --> 00:15:05.568
which is the address-to-asset
record NIST asks for.

193
00:15:05.568 --> 00:15:11.054
Dynamic DNS updates from DHCP are
something you turn on, not automatic.

194
00:15:11.054 --> 00:15:11.905
IPAM.

195
00:15:11.905 --> 00:15:16.355
A network view is a single routing
domain, up to one thousand per Grid.

196
00:15:16.355 --> 00:15:21.217
Views are mutually exclusive, so the same
address space can exist in two of them.

197
00:15:21.217 --> 00:15:24.942
A DHCP member serves
only one network view.

198
00:15:24.942 --> 00:15:27.522
Network containers hold the large blocks,

199
00:15:27.522 --> 00:15:32.755
and extensible attributes tag objects with
metadata such as division or cost center.

200
00:15:32.755 --> 00:15:36.326
Discovery uses ICMP, NetBIOS or TCP,

201
00:15:36.326 --> 00:15:41.868
and vDiscovery reads vCenter,
OpenStack, Azure and AWS.

202
00:15:41.868 --> 00:15:47.169
Network Insight, the layer-two and
layer-three device discovery, needs its

203
00:15:47.169 --> 00:15:52.643
own license and dedicated appliances that
run no DNS or DHCP; budget for that.

204
00:15:52.643 --> 00:15:56.617
Universal Asset Insights is
the SaaS inventory service.

205
00:15:56.617 --> 00:16:00.543
And AWS documents Infoblox
as the external authority

206
00:16:00.543 --> 00:16:04.206
for a VPC IPAM private
scope, on the Advanced tier.

207
00:16:04.206 --> 00:16:06.505
Two corrections to common claims.

208
00:16:06.505 --> 00:16:10.317
Separate network views permit
overlap; they don't prevent it.

209
00:16:10.317 --> 00:16:14.818
And the AWS IPAM integration
excludes the GovCloud regions.

210
00:16:14.818 --> 00:16:21.241
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x: DHCP
Failover, DHCPv6 RFC Compliance,

211
00:16:21.241 --> 00:16:29.846
DHCP Fingerprint Detection, Lease History,
Enabling DDNS, Configuring Network Views,

212
00:16:29.846 --> 00:16:36.270
About Discovery, About Network
Insight, Extensible Attributes;

213
00:16:36.270 --> 00:16:43.178
Universal Asset Insights; AWS VPC
IPAM user guide; AWS What's New, Nov.

214
00:16:43.178 --> 00:16:44.260
17, 2025.

215
00:16:45.010 --> 00:16:49.401
Infoblox Threat Defense,
formerly BloxOne Threat Defense,

216
00:16:49.401 --> 00:16:51.910
is the DNS-layer security product.

217
00:16:51.910 --> 00:16:56.466
It bundles three things: DNS Firewall
using response policy zones, TIDE,

218
00:16:56.466 --> 00:16:59.014
the Threat Intelligence Data Exchange,

219
00:16:59.014 --> 00:17:04.111
which aggregates and distributes
machine-readable intelligence, and Dossier,

220
00:17:04.111 --> 00:17:08.435
which researches an indicator across
about a dozen sources at once.

221
00:17:08.435 --> 00:17:11.866
It detects DNS tunneling
and data exfiltration,

222
00:17:11.866 --> 00:17:16.497
domain generation algorithms,
command-and-control and phishing.

223
00:17:16.497 --> 00:17:18.135
It's delivered two ways.

224
00:17:18.135 --> 00:17:21.357
From the cloud, through
the DNS Forwarding Proxy,

225
00:17:21.357 --> 00:17:24.660
the Infoblox Endpoint
agent and a cloud resolver.

226
00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:29.548
And on premises, as RPZ
feeds into NIOS or NIOS-X.

227
00:17:29.548 --> 00:17:33.228
On NIOS, Threat Insight
detects tunneling and adds

228
00:17:33.228 --> 00:17:36.997
the destination to a
mitigation RPZ automatically.

229
00:17:36.997 --> 00:17:42.753
Threat Defense Detection Mode streams DNS
reply metadata from NIOS to the cloud for

230
00:17:42.753 --> 00:17:47.110
passive detection; that metadata
includes client IP addresses,

231
00:17:47.110 --> 00:17:50.398
so treat it as a boundary
decision, not a checkbox.

232
00:17:50.398 --> 00:17:53.489
Licensing comes in four tiers: Essentials,

233
00:17:53.489 --> 00:17:57.198
Business On-Premises,
Business Cloud and Advanced.

234
00:17:57.198 --> 00:18:01.447
The cloud delivery pieces come
with Business Cloud and Advanced.

235
00:18:01.447 --> 00:18:04.710
Custom feed ingest in
TIDE is Advanced only.

236
00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:07.850
Dossier gives thirty-two
thousand queries a year on

237
00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:11.210
the Business tiers and
sixty-four thousand on Advanced.

238
00:18:11.210 --> 00:18:13.648
There's one strong independent signal.

239
00:18:13.648 --> 00:18:17.203
Google Cloud's own
documentation describes its

240
00:18:17.203 --> 00:18:20.935
DNS Armor threat detection
as powered by Infoblox.

241
00:18:20.935 --> 00:18:24.522
That's a cloud provider's
statement, not Infoblox marketing.

242
00:18:24.522 --> 00:18:25.897
And then there's the marketing.

243
00:18:25.897 --> 00:18:30.947
Infoblox says it stops ninety percent
of threats before the first query,

244
00:18:30.947 --> 00:18:34.650
detects threats sixty-eight
days before other tools,

245
00:18:34.650 --> 00:18:37.848
and has a false-positive
rate of 0.0002 percent.

246
00:18:37.848 --> 00:18:40.861
Nobody independent has
verified those figures.

247
00:18:40.861 --> 00:18:43.285
Keep them out of anything
an assessor reads.

248
00:18:43.285 --> 00:18:49.454
Sources: Infoblox About Threat
Defense; Licensing and Subscriptions;

249
00:18:49.454 --> 00:18:55.622
Dossier and TIDE Overview; About Threat
Insight (NIOS 9.1.x); TDDM for NIOS;

250
00:18:55.622 --> 00:19:02.680
Google Cloud DNS Armor docs; infoblox.com
Threat Defense page (vendor claims).

251
00:19:03.430 --> 00:19:06.318
Automation and logging,
the two things that decide

252
00:19:06.318 --> 00:19:08.930
whether the platform can
be governed at scale.

253
00:19:08.930 --> 00:19:13.368
On automation, Infoblox is
ahead of the other DDI vendors.

254
00:19:13.368 --> 00:19:20.629
NIOS's REST API, WAPI, shows version
2.14 in the 9.1.0 examples, and 9.1 adds

255
00:19:20.629 --> 00:19:28.280
OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect sign-in
for the API and immutable object IDs.

256
00:19:28.280 --> 00:19:33.218
Universal DDI has its own
REST API with API-key tokens.

257
00:19:33.218 --> 00:19:37.142
There's also a WAPI-compatible
API for Universal DDI,

258
00:19:37.142 --> 00:19:41.979
but Infoblox says it's for migration
only and isn't a long-term API.

259
00:19:41.979 --> 00:19:44.019
For infrastructure as code,

260
00:19:44.019 --> 00:19:49.517
the infobloxopen/infoblox Terraform
provider went to a 3.0 beta on October

261
00:19:49.517 --> 00:19:54.926
thirteenth: one provider for both NIOS
and Universal DDI, replacing three

262
00:19:54.926 --> 00:19:59.980
older ones, and not backward compatible,
so state has to be re-imported.

263
00:19:59.980 --> 00:20:04.055
Plan that migration; don't build
new work on the older providers.

264
00:20:04.055 --> 00:20:08.396
Ansible has current collections
for both, and ServiceNow publishes

265
00:20:08.396 --> 00:20:12.280
an Infoblox spoke that needs
IntegrationHub and a MID Server.

266
00:20:12.280 --> 00:20:13.968
Logging has sharper edges.

267
00:20:13.968 --> 00:20:18.682
Query and response capture rolls files
every ten minutes or hundred megabytes,

268
00:20:18.682 --> 00:20:22.396
with local retention capped by
model, and Infoblox recommends

269
00:20:22.396 --> 00:20:26.181
not logging queries and responses
together because of the load.

270
00:20:26.181 --> 00:20:29.794
dnstap runs on BIND in 9.1,
but drops logs rather than

271
00:20:29.794 --> 00:20:34.268
buffering them if the receiver is
down, so monitor the receiver.

272
00:20:34.268 --> 00:20:36.980
RPZ hits go to syslog in CEF.

273
00:20:36.980 --> 00:20:41.793
Cloud logs reach Splunk or Microsoft
Sentinel through the Data Connector;

274
00:20:41.793 --> 00:20:44.355
Splunk's own Infoblox add-on is archived.

275
00:20:44.355 --> 00:20:48.421
And the built-in Reporting
appliances have daily ingest caps:

276
00:20:48.421 --> 00:20:51.530
ten gigabytes on the
largest, five on the others.

277
00:20:51.530 --> 00:20:54.435
Exceed the cap five
times in thirty days and

278
00:20:54.435 --> 00:20:57.743
reporting locks until
Infoblox Support steps in.

279
00:20:57.743 --> 00:21:02.529
For federal retention, send logs to the
SIEM and size Reporting deliberately.

280
00:21:02.529 --> 00:21:07.893
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.0 What's
New; Universal DDI API Guide;

281
00:21:07.893 --> 00:21:14.973
WAPI 2.0 for Universal DDI; infobloxopen
terraform-provider-infoblox releases;

282
00:21:14.973 --> 00:21:21.409
infoblox-ansible and universal-ddi-ansible;
ServiceNow Infoblox spoke;

283
00:21:21.409 --> 00:21:25.592
DNS Query and Response
Captures; dnstap limits;

284
00:21:25.592 --> 00:21:31.860
Data Connector; Supported Reporting
Appliances; Splunkbase 2934.

285
00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:35.675
Module 2: why Infoblox
is a top recommendation,

286
00:21:35.675 --> 00:21:38.498
and why federal networks
need what it does.

287
00:21:38.498 --> 00:21:40.548
We'll build the case in four steps.

288
00:21:40.548 --> 00:21:43.996
First, map each federal
requirement to the capability

289
00:21:43.996 --> 00:21:47.522
it demands and to the Infoblox
feature that delivers it.

290
00:21:47.522 --> 00:21:50.476
Second, look closely at
what the FedRAMP listing

291
00:21:50.476 --> 00:21:53.798
covers and what stays inside
the agency's own boundary.

292
00:21:53.798 --> 00:21:58.469
Third, compare the assurance evidence
and the features against BlueCat,

293
00:21:58.469 --> 00:22:01.973
EfficientIP, Microsoft and
the native cloud services.

294
00:22:01.973 --> 00:22:04.354
And fourth, lay out the risks plainly,

295
00:22:04.354 --> 00:22:08.323
with how to manage each one and
what to ask for in the contract.

296
00:22:08.323 --> 00:22:13.360
A recommendation that hides its risks
isn't one an authorizing official can use.

297
00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:20.711
Sources: FedRAMP Marketplace
products.json; DISA STIG file store;

298
00:22:20.711 --> 00:22:26.120
NIST CMVP; NIAP PCL; CISA
CDM APL; NVD; CISA KEV.

299
00:22:26.870 --> 00:22:31.718
This is the core of the argument: each
requirement, the capability it demands,

300
00:22:31.718 --> 00:22:35.257
the Infoblox feature that
delivers it, and what to watch.

301
00:22:35.257 --> 00:22:39.931
Signing agency zones: the Grid
Master signs as a hidden primary,

302
00:22:39.931 --> 00:22:43.795
rolls keys automatically,
and can keep keys in an HSM.

303
00:22:43.795 --> 00:22:47.119
Watch the RSA-SHA1
default and the DS record

304
00:22:47.119 --> 00:22:50.258
you'll still hand to
the dot-gov registrar.

305
00:22:50.258 --> 00:22:54.929
Resilient, role-separated DNS:
Grid replication, HA pairs,

306
00:22:54.929 --> 00:23:00.295
a Grid Master Candidate, anycast,
and internal and external views.

307
00:23:00.295 --> 00:23:05.370
Watch that candidate promotion is manual,
so it belongs in a tested runbook.

308
00:23:05.370 --> 00:23:09.368
Encrypted DNS at agency
resolvers: from 9.1,

309
00:23:09.368 --> 00:23:13.708
BIND serves DoT and DoH,
but on X6 hardware only.

310
00:23:13.708 --> 00:23:16.718
Protective DNS: per-view forwarders send

311
00:23:16.718 --> 00:23:20.171
egress to CISA while the
Grid keeps local logs.

312
00:23:20.171 --> 00:23:21.220
Watch one thing.

313
00:23:21.220 --> 00:23:26.584
CISA's FAQ notes that some vendors don't
support the protocols for forwarding to its

314
00:23:26.584 --> 00:23:30.874
resolver, and our research found
no Infoblox document on encrypted

315
00:23:30.874 --> 00:23:34.782
forwarding to CISA, so confirm it
in a lab before you rely on it.

316
00:23:34.782 --> 00:23:37.803
Logging: query capture,
dnstap, lease history,

317
00:23:37.803 --> 00:23:42.374
CEF syslog and the Data Connector
cover what the Logging Reference

318
00:23:42.374 --> 00:23:46.945
Architecture maps; watch the ingest
caps and dnstap's drop behavior.

319
00:23:46.945 --> 00:23:52.198
Inventory for BOD 23-01, BOD
26-04 and CM-8: IPAM, discovery,

320
00:23:52.198 --> 00:23:59.351
cloud vDiscovery and Asset Insights
give the authoritative address record;

321
00:23:59.351 --> 00:24:04.045
watch the dedicated hardware
for Network Insight.

322
00:24:04.045 --> 00:24:13.545
IPv6: IPv6 Grids, DHCPv6 and prefix
delegation, but DHCP failover is IPv4 only.

323
00:24:13.545 --> 00:24:18.358
And validated cryptography is
the gap: Infoblox's FIPS 140-3

324
00:24:18.358 --> 00:24:23.270
modules are in NIST review, with
no active certificate today.

325
00:24:23.270 --> 00:24:26.765
Read across the rows:
seven of eight requirements

326
00:24:26.765 --> 00:24:29.408
land on one platform and one database.

327
00:24:29.408 --> 00:24:33.064
That consolidation is the case
for a governed DDI plane,

328
00:24:33.064 --> 00:24:35.846
and for Infoblox as a
strong way to build it.

329
00:24:35.846 --> 00:24:47.236
Sources: NIST SP 800-53r5; OMB
M-08-23, M-22-09, M-21-07; EO 14144;

330
00:24:47.236 --> 00:24:55.372
CISA Protective DNS FAQ;
CISA LRA; BOD 23-01, 26-04;

331
00:24:55.372 --> 00:25:07.540
Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x and Universal DDI
docs; NIST CMVP modules in process.

332
00:25:08.290 --> 00:25:12.348
FedRAMP is the most misquoted
part of any Infoblox proposal,

333
00:25:12.348 --> 00:25:13.890
so let's read the record.

334
00:25:13.890 --> 00:25:20.336
The Marketplace lists Infoblox
Government Cloud, package FR2017257053,

335
00:25:20.336 --> 00:25:26.888
as FedRAMP Certified at Moderate, which
the Marketplace now labels Class C,

336
00:25:26.888 --> 00:25:31.115
on the agency path, as SaaS
hosted in AWS GovCloud.

337
00:25:31.115 --> 00:25:36.953
It was certified in December 2022 through
the agency path, with Kratos as assessor.

338
00:25:36.953 --> 00:25:40.890
It shows one agency authorization
and a reuse count of zero.

339
00:25:40.890 --> 00:25:45.440
There's no High listing, and we
couldn't verify any DoD impact level.

340
00:25:45.440 --> 00:25:46.478
What's in scope?

341
00:25:46.478 --> 00:25:50.560
The listing names Universal
DDI, Universal Asset Insights,

342
00:25:50.560 --> 00:25:54.727
and the Threat Defense component
services TIDE and Dossier.

343
00:25:54.727 --> 00:25:59.961
Infoblox's July 2026 blog says the
offering now includes NIOS-X servers;

344
00:25:59.961 --> 00:26:03.864
treat that as a vendor claim
until the package says so.

345
00:26:03.864 --> 00:26:07.149
What isn't named: the Threat
Defense cloud resolver,

346
00:26:07.149 --> 00:26:10.277
the DNS Forwarding Proxy
and the Data Connector.

347
00:26:10.277 --> 00:26:15.253
Ask Infoblox for the authorization boundary
diagram before you design around them.

348
00:26:15.253 --> 00:26:18.836
And the biggest point: NIOS
appliances and vNIOS running

349
00:26:18.836 --> 00:26:22.266
on agency infrastructure
aren't a cloud service at all.

350
00:26:22.266 --> 00:26:24.590
No FedRAMP authorization covers them.

351
00:26:24.590 --> 00:26:29.557
They sit inside the agency's own
boundary and its own ATO, so a statement

352
00:26:29.557 --> 00:26:34.953
like "Infoblox's FedRAMP authorization
covers the DDI layer" is an overclaim.

353
00:26:34.953 --> 00:26:39.066
For High or DoD systems, the
path is agency-operated vNIOS,

354
00:26:39.066 --> 00:26:44.753
for example from the Azure Government
Marketplace, which Infoblox documents.

355
00:26:44.753 --> 00:26:46.215
Two practical notes.

356
00:26:46.215 --> 00:26:49.295
With reuse at zero,
expect to do your own ATO

357
00:26:49.295 --> 00:26:52.640
work rather than ride a
widely reused package.

358
00:26:52.640 --> 00:26:57.251
And Infoblox keeps separate FedRAMP
release notes whose latest entry is March

359
00:26:57.251 --> 00:27:01.163
fourteenth, while the commercial
notes run to October seventeenth,

360
00:27:01.163 --> 00:27:04.865
which suggests the FedRAMP
instance lags commercial features.

361
00:27:04.865 --> 00:27:06.465
Ask for the parity list.

362
00:27:06.465 --> 00:27:15.600
Sources: FedRAMP Marketplace
products.json, FR2017257053 (accessed Oct.

363
00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:20.777
22, 2026); Infoblox blog,
July 22, 2026 (vendor);

364
00:27:20.777 --> 00:27:27.980
What's New in Universal DDI
for FedRAMP; vNIOS for Azure.

365
00:27:28.730 --> 00:27:33.586
A narrower question: if an agency buys
Trinzic, the physical NIOS appliances,

366
00:27:33.586 --> 00:27:36.592
how well does the hardware
fit the design in the

367
00:27:36.592 --> 00:27:40.292
Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 08 of the workbook?

368
00:27:40.292 --> 00:27:45.046
One authoritative policy plane: the
Grid gives you one shared database,

369
00:27:45.046 --> 00:27:48.268
an HA Grid Master and a
candidate with a full copy.

370
00:27:48.268 --> 00:27:50.600
But candidate promotion is manual,

371
00:27:50.600 --> 00:27:55.267
and Infoblox publishes no Grid scale
limits, so size it with the vendor.

372
00:27:55.267 --> 00:27:58.573
Leaving Active Directory–integrated DNS:

373
00:27:58.573 --> 00:28:04.429
GSS-TSIG lets domain controllers send
Kerberos-signed updates to the Grid.

374
00:28:04.429 --> 00:28:08.573
But an appliance can send
GSS-TSIG updates or accept them,

375
00:28:08.573 --> 00:28:11.393
not both, so plan which members do which.

376
00:28:11.393 --> 00:28:14.955
DNSSEC signing is central,
on the Grid Master,

377
00:28:14.955 --> 00:28:19.480
with keys there or in a Thales
Luna or Entrust nShield HSM.

378
00:28:19.480 --> 00:28:22.380
Change the RSA SHA-1 default.

379
00:28:22.380 --> 00:28:26.343
Encrypted DNS is served by BIND from 9.1,

380
00:28:26.343 --> 00:28:30.430
free only on X6; X5
needs an extra license.

381
00:28:30.430 --> 00:28:35.283
IPAM and basic discovery are built
in, but Network Insight needs a

382
00:28:35.283 --> 00:28:40.830
Discovery license and dedicated ND-series
appliances that run no DNS or DHCP.

383
00:28:40.830 --> 00:28:43.849
Reporting appliances
have daily ingest caps;

384
00:28:43.849 --> 00:28:48.218
five overruns in thirty days lock
reporting, so send logs to the SIEM.

385
00:28:48.218 --> 00:28:52.379
Trinzic sits inside the
agency's own boundary and ATO.

386
00:28:52.379 --> 00:28:55.130
The FedRAMP Moderate
listing doesn't cover it,

387
00:28:55.130 --> 00:28:57.518
which is why it's the
path for High and DoD.

388
00:28:57.518 --> 00:29:04.399
Assurance and lifecycle: certificate 4531
for Trinzic hardware went Historical in

389
00:29:04.399 --> 00:29:09.314
September, the FIPS 140-3 Trinzic
modules are pending review,

390
00:29:09.314 --> 00:29:15.605
and NIAP has been evaluating Trinzic
appliances with NIOS 9.0 since August 17.

391
00:29:15.605 --> 00:29:19.868
X6 needs NIOS 9.0.1 or later.

392
00:29:19.868 --> 00:29:25.054
Hardware is typically supported about
four years after its last order date,

393
00:29:25.054 --> 00:29:30.241
and an internet-facing appliance past
end of support may fall under BOD 26-02,

394
00:29:30.241 --> 00:29:32.793
though it doesn't name DNS appliances.

395
00:29:32.793 --> 00:29:39.949
Sources: Infoblox NIOS 9.1.x: Promoting
a Grid Master Candidate, About Grids,

396
00:29:39.949 --> 00:29:46.872
About GSS-TSIG, Configuring DNSSEC on
a Grid, Setting DNSSEC Parameters,

397
00:29:46.872 --> 00:29:53.090
Thales Luna HSM, DoT and DoH
Architecture, About Network Insight,

398
00:29:53.090 --> 00:29:58.370
Supported Reporting Appliances;
NIOS 9.1.0 blog (vendor);

399
00:29:58.370 --> 00:30:04.589
NIST CMVP certificate 4531 and
Modules In Process list; NIAP PCL;

400
00:30:04.589 --> 00:30:11.990
Infoblox End-of-Life policy; CISA BOD
26-02; FedRAMP products.json; Book 08.

401
00:30:12.740 --> 00:30:16.014
So can Trinzic run an
agency's DDI alone once

402
00:30:16.014 --> 00:30:19.378
workloads move to AWS,
Azure and Google Cloud?

403
00:30:19.378 --> 00:30:22.228
On its own, it can see into the clouds.

404
00:30:22.228 --> 00:30:27.071
vDiscovery pulls in cloud
networks and VMs on a schedule,

405
00:30:27.071 --> 00:30:33.565
and NIOS can import Route 53, Azure DNS
and Google Cloud DNS zones, one way only.

406
00:30:33.565 --> 00:30:35.753
What it can't do is own cloud DNS.

407
00:30:35.753 --> 00:30:38.378
Changes made in NIOS don't write back,

408
00:30:38.378 --> 00:30:41.528
and it can't create the
clouds' private zones.

409
00:30:41.528 --> 00:30:45.674
No NIOS document describes
managing Route 53 VPC

410
00:30:45.674 --> 00:30:50.327
Resolver rules or Azure DNS
Private Resolver rulesets.

411
00:30:50.327 --> 00:30:53.596
It isn't the authority for AWS VPC IPAM,

412
00:30:53.596 --> 00:30:57.953
and DHCP in cloud subnets
stays with the cloud fabric.

413
00:30:57.953 --> 00:31:00.878
There are three ways to
extend it, and they combine.

414
00:31:00.878 --> 00:31:05.142
Option A puts vNIOS members in
each cloud, joined to the Grid,

415
00:31:05.142 --> 00:31:09.841
Azure Government included, so
answers stay local during a WAN cut.

416
00:31:09.841 --> 00:31:12.747
Option B joins the Grid to Universal DDI,

417
00:31:12.747 --> 00:31:18.562
the only Infoblox path that writes back
to cloud DNS, but the government edition

418
00:31:18.562 --> 00:31:24.553
is Moderate only, with limited discovery:
single AWS accounts and no Azure tenants.

419
00:31:24.553 --> 00:31:29.490
Its cloud forwarders run on NIOS-X
servers, not classic Trinzic.

420
00:31:29.490 --> 00:31:32.882
Option C keeps the native
cloud resolvers and wires them

421
00:31:32.882 --> 00:31:36.490
to the Grid with conditional
forwarding in both directions.

422
00:31:36.490 --> 00:31:42.415
And the AWS VPC IPAM handoff to
Infoblox isn't available in GovCloud.

423
00:31:42.415 --> 00:31:45.328
The bottom row is design
guidance, not a rule.

424
00:31:45.328 --> 00:31:47.912
Mostly on premises: Option C.

425
00:31:47.912 --> 00:31:50.608
Hybrid with one cloud: A plus C.

426
00:31:50.608 --> 00:31:54.090
Multi-cloud at Moderate or below: add B.

427
00:31:54.090 --> 00:31:57.909
GovCloud, Azure Government, FedRAMP High,

428
00:31:57.909 --> 00:32:02.290
or IL4 and IL5: A plus C,
and don't depend on the SaaS.

429
00:32:02.290 --> 00:32:05.762
For the full reasoning,
see the Research explainer,

430
00:32:05.762 --> 00:32:08.490
Trinzic Alone, or Trinzic Plus Cloud DDI?

431
00:32:08.490 --> 00:32:15.574
